<h3>The 70 / 20 / 10 rule</h3>
<p>Seventy percent warm whitewash and sand — walls, ceilings, drapery, base upholstery, floors where applicable. Twenty percent one accent tone chosen from terracotta, olive, sky blue, or deep sea — used on one wall, one furniture piece, cushions, art, and tile accents. Ten percent matte black — hardware, curtain rods, light fixture frames, framing detail. Follow this rule and the palette will always resolve. Break it and the room becomes bohemian.</p>
<h4>Palette combinations that work reliably</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Whitewash + Warm Sand + Deep Sea Blue + Wrought Black</strong> — the coastal-leaning version. Best for open-plan living-dining and master bedrooms.</li>
  <li><strong>Whitewash + Terracotta + Olive + Wrought Black</strong> — the Mediterranean-leaning version. Excellent for kitchens and dining rooms.</li>
  <li><strong>Whitewash + Driftwood + Sky Blue + Wrought Black</strong> — the softest, most airy version. Ideal for compact apartments where the accent must not overwhelm.</li>
  <li><strong>Whitewash + Warm Sand + Terracotta + Wrought Black</strong> — the warmest version. Best for homes with cooler natural exposures.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><span class="callout__title">Avoid at all costs</span>Pure white walls (cold under LED), bright royal blue (turns nautical-costume), lime green (turns tropical), grey-based whites (drains warmth), and any polished gold or bright brass accent (immediately shifts the style toward Deco or Modern Classic). One polished gold tap can kill the whole room.</div>
<p>05 · Materials &amp; Finishes</p>
<h2>The material shortlist and what to spend on each</h2>
<p>Mediterranean-Coastal is a material-honest style. Materials do more of the aesthetic work here than in almost any other style, which means material selection is where a design manager spends most of their attention. Here is the priority hierarchy for Bangalore homes in 2026.</p>
<h3>Lime plaster or textured paint on walls</h3>
<p>The single most important material specification in this style. True Venetian or lime plaster is applied over multiple coats to produce a slightly uneven, soft-shadowed wall surface. In Bangalore, Asian Paints Royale Play Lime Wash and Berger Silk Illusions Suede finishes replicate the effect at ₹90–₹180 per square foot including labour, versus real Venetian plaster at ₹350–₹650. Both are legitimate — real lime is superior visually and lasts longer; the textured paint is a workable compromise below premium tier. Flat matte gyp-board finish is the wrong choice for this style.</p>
<h3>Wood — teak, mango, and reclaimed hardwood</h3>
<p>The signature Mediterranean-Coastal wood species are teak (rich warm brown, ages beautifully), mango (paler, more affordable, similar honest grain), and reclaimed hardwood (character marks, patina, sustainability story). All should be finished in <strong>matte oil or unlacquered wax finish</strong> — never high-gloss lacquer. Solid wood beams, floating open shelves, live-edge dining tables, and chunky dining benches carry the style. Expect ₹380–₹850 per sq ft for solid teak furniture; ₹220–₹450 for mango; ₹500–₹1,100 for reclaimed with sourcing.</p>
<h3>Natural stone — limestone, travertine, sandstone, terracotta</h3>
<p>Real natural stone is more Mediterranean than any other material choice. Limestone and travertine (soft cream tones with subtle veining) for floors, countertops, and vanity slabs. Sandstone (Jaisalmer yellow, Dholpur red) for feature walls, jaali screens, and outdoor thresholds. Terracotta floor tiles (handmade, unglazed) for kitchens, foyers, and powder rooms. All three age into their finish rather than out of it. Rates: limestone/travertine ₹180–₹380/sqft supplied; terracotta ₹120–₹220/sqft; sandstone ₹80–₹180/sqft.</p>
<h3>Handmade tile — Zellige, encaustic, cement</h3>
<p>Handmade ceramic and cement tiles are the highest-return low-cost intervention in this style. Zellige (Moroccan handmade glazed ceramic) at ₹350–₹850 per sq ft, encaustic cement tiles (patterned handmade) at ₹280–₹680, and hand-cut cement tiles at ₹180–₹380. One 40-sqft feature wall of Zellige in the kitchen or powder room transforms the entire home. Buy from Bharat Floorings, Tunis Tiles, or specialist importers.</p>
<h3>Linen, cotton, and jute upholstery</h3>
<p>Natural fibres carry Mediterranean-Coastal upholstery. Belgian linen for sofas and drapery, heavyweight cotton canvas for slipcovers, jute for rugs and cushion backs. No synthetic velvets, no polyester blends. Real linen upholstery from D'Decor's natural range or Sarom starts at ₹1,200 per metre and reaches ₹3,500 per metre for imported Belgian. Yes, real linen wrinkles — that's the point. Homes that try to keep linen crease-free by ironing weekly have missed the aesthetic.</p>
<h3>Rattan, cane, and seagrass</h3>
<p>Woven natural fibres appear in furniture (cane-back chairs, rattan lounge chairs), lighting (rattan pendant shades), cabinet fronts (cane-inlay wardrobes and vanity fronts), and accessories (jute rugs, seagrass baskets). Restraint matters — use in three places per home, not everywhere. Excess reads as tropical resort, not sophisticated coastal.</p>
<h3>Matte black wrought iron</h3>
<p>Every metal in the home in matte black. Hardware, tapware, curtain rods, light fixtures, mirror frames, chandelier arms, staircase railings, garden furniture. Powder-coated iron from Ozone, Hafele's matte black range, or custom-fabricated at any Bangalore metal workshop. This one material choice defines the style's authenticity. There is <strong>no room for brass, gold, or bright chrome</strong> in Mediterranean-Coastal.</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Design manager's material tip</span>If your budget allows only three material upgrades over generic contemporary, spend them here: (1) textured or lime plaster on walls instead of flat paint, (2) matte black hardware throughout instead of chrome or brushed steel, (3) one handmade tile feature wall in the kitchen or powder room. These three interventions cost approximately ₹1.4–₹2.1 lakh combined on a 3BHK and move the entire home from "contemporary" to "Mediterranean-Coastal."</div>
<p>06 · Furniture Style</p>
<h2>Silhouettes that relax the room, not perform it</h2>
<p>Mediterranean-Coastal furniture is defined by <em>relaxed proportions</em>. Deep seating, generous cushioning, low profiles, chunky wood, and honest joinery. There is no place in this style for tight tailoring, sharp geometric edges, or high-gloss finishes. The furniture should invite bare feet and long afternoons.</p>
<h3>Living room essentials</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Deep linen slipcover sofa</strong> — usually 3-seater, in warm ivory or oatmeal linen, with removable washable covers. Seat height 40–43 cm (lower than contemporary), seat depth 62–70 cm (deeper than contemporary).</li>
  <li><strong>Rattan or cane-back accent chairs</strong> — pair of woven-back chairs with cotton or linen cushions in warm neutral.</li>
  <li><strong>Chunky wood coffee table</strong> — solid teak or mango slab, rectangular or oval, on plinth or four straight legs. 110–140 cm long.</li>
  <li><strong>Open shelving unit</strong> — solid wood floating shelves or a freestanding ladder shelf, styled with books, ceramics, and small plants.</li>
  <li><strong>Woven jute or wool rug</strong> — natural fibre, oversized (should extend under all seating).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Dining room essentials</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Live-edge or refectory-style dining table</strong> — solid teak or mango, matte oil finish, sized for household plus two guests.</li>
  <li><strong>Wishbone or cross-back chairs</strong> — solid wood, cane seat, matte finish. Or upholstered linen dining chairs for a softer look.</li>
  <li><strong>Wooden sideboard</strong> — solid wood, matte black hardware, open shelving section for display.</li>
  <li><strong>Wood-bead or rattan chandelier</strong> above the table.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Bedroom essentials</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Upholstered linen headboard</strong> — soft rectangular or arched, oatmeal or ivory linen, floor-height or extending above the mattress by 100–120 cm (does not need to be floor-to-ceiling in this style).</li>
  <li><strong>Solid wood bed frame</strong> — low platform in matte teak or mango, exposed grain.</li>
  <li><strong>Nightstands</strong> — solid wood with cane inlay or matte black hardware, or a small round wooden side table.</li>
  <li><strong>Rattan pendant lights</strong> or matte black wrought iron sconces beside the bed.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">A note on proportion</span>Mediterranean-Coastal furniture is <em>heavier and more grounded</em> than contemporary furniture but not as ornamental as traditional. Silhouettes lean casual — think weekend house, not urban formal. If a piece looks like it belongs in a boardroom or a five-star hotel lobby, it does not belong in this style.</div>
<p>07 · Kitchen Design</p>
<h2>Where the style delivers its highest visible return</h2>
<p>Kitchens are the room where Mediterranean-Coastal offers the best value ratio of any premium interior style. A modular kitchen executed in this style at ₹3,200 per running foot looks more expensive and more distinctive than a generic contemporary kitchen at ₹4,500 per running foot. The materials are cheaper. The visual return is higher. This asymmetry is the reason we recommend the style disproportionately for kitchen-heavy homes.</p>
<h3>The formula that works</h3>
<p>Matte off-white or cream cabinetry, natural wood open shelving, handmade tile backsplash, honed natural stone counter, matte black hardware, rattan or wood pendants over the island or peninsula. This is the entire recipe. Everything else is variation.</p>
<h3>Signature Mediterranean-Coastal kitchen elements</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Open shelving instead of upper cabinets</strong> — solid wood shelves (18–25 mm thick) on matte black wrought iron brackets, replacing at least half of the upper cabinets. Displays ceramic dinnerware, glass jars, cookbooks. Higher aesthetic return than any other kitchen intervention.</li>
  <li><strong>Handmade tile backsplash</strong> — Zellige, encaustic, or hand-cut cement tile as a full-height backsplash. Whitewash Zellige for the airiest version; terracotta or blue-and-white patterned encaustic for more drama.</li>
  <li><strong>Honed natural stone counter</strong> — limestone, travertine, or honed marble. Matte finish only — polished surfaces read as generic contemporary.</li>
  <li><strong>Arched pantry or niche</strong> — an arched wall opening leading to a pantry room, or an arched niche behind the hob for oils and daily jars.</li>
  <li><strong>Wood-slat or rattan pendant lights</strong> over the island or breakfast counter — the single largest visual mood-setter.</li>
  <li><strong>Farmhouse-style single-bowl sink</strong> — deep ceramic or fireclay apron-front sink, replacing standard under-mount stainless steel.</li>
  <li><strong>Matte black hardware</strong> — every knob, pull, tap, and drain cover in matte black. No exceptions.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Layouts that work best</h3>
<p>L-shape and U-shape kitchens both work well. Island kitchens shine when the space permits — the island becomes the room's hero. Straight galley kitchens work but lose 30% of the visual impact because there's no place to anchor a statement pendant. G-shape kitchens with a peninsula that doubles as a breakfast counter are ideal for standard 3BHK Bangalore apartments.</p>
