<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Bangalore-specific note</span>Handmade Zellige tile is highly water-absorbent and needs sealing before grouting. Insist on the tile supplier providing the specific sealer with the delivery, and on the installer applying it before the tiles go up. This one 4-hour step prevents the Zellige from developing water stains within 6 months — a common failure we see when clients source tiles from generalist suppliers.</div>
<p>08 · Bedroom Design</p>
<h2>Restful, breezy, and lower-cost than most premium bedroom styles</h2>
<p>Mediterranean-Coastal bedrooms are among the most restful of any interior style. They also cost meaningfully less to execute than Modern Classic or Art Deco Revival bedrooms because the material palette is inherently cheaper — linen instead of velvet, natural wood instead of veneer over solid wood, textured paint instead of feature panelling. This is a bedroom style that scales well from ₹1.2 lakh (entry) to ₹4 lakh (premium) for a full master bedroom.</p>
<h3>The formula</h3>
<p>Whitewash walls (three sides), one soft accent wall (either textured lime plaster in a warm sand tone, or wood-slat panelling in matte teak, or Zellige tile in a small central panel behind the headboard). Upholstered linen headboard, solid wood bed frame, natural fibre rug, drapery in ivory linen, matte black wrought iron sconces, one rattan pendant or ceiling fan with wood-blade finish.</p>
<h3>Signature Mediterranean-Coastal bedroom elements</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Soft upholstered headboard in ivory or oatmeal linen</strong> — arched or rectangular with piped edging. Height 130–160 cm total (not floor-to-ceiling; that's a different style).</li>
  <li><strong>Solid wood platform bed</strong> — matte teak or mango with visible grain, low profile with 12–18 cm plinth base.</li>
  <li><strong>Natural fibre rug</strong> — jute, seagrass, or wool in a warm neutral. Extends 60 cm beyond the bed on three sides.</li>
  <li><strong>Wardrobe with cane inlay or wood-slat fronts</strong> — matte finish, matte black long-pulls, no glossy surfaces.</li>
  <li><strong>Wooden bench at the foot of the bed</strong> or a large seagrass basket for laundry.</li>
  <li><strong>Matte black wrought iron sconces</strong> at 140 cm above the floor, flanking the bed. Or single rattan pendant on each side.</li>
  <li><strong>Ceiling fan with wood-finish blades</strong> or matte-black frame — this is India; the ceiling fan is the aesthetic elephant in every bedroom and must match the style.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">The ceiling fan problem, solved</span>Every Mediterranean-Coastal bedroom in Bangalore needs a ceiling fan. Standard white or chrome ceiling fans kill the aesthetic. Options that actually work: (1) Havells Stealth Wooden or Atomberg Renesa wooden-blade — ₹6,000–₹9,000; (2) matte black frame fans from Fanzart or Casablanca India — ₹12,000–₹22,000; (3) rattan-blade fans from specialist suppliers — ₹15,000–₹35,000. Spend on this. It's the single most visible element in a bedroom for 12 hours a day.</div>
<p>09 · Living Room Design</p>
<h2>Casual comfort, natural materials, and an emphasis on breathability</h2>
<p>Mediterranean-Coastal living rooms are among the most family-friendly of any premium style. They accommodate real daily use — kids on the sofa, dogs on the rug, feet on the coffee table — because the materials are honest and forgiving. Linen slipcovers wash. Solid wood scratches and gains character. Jute rugs absorb spills. This is a living room style designed for lived-in use, not just for guests.</p>
<h3>The anchor arrangement</h3>
<p>Deep linen slipcover sofa (3-seater in warm ivory), pair of rattan or cane-back accent chairs facing it, chunky solid wood coffee table between, oversized natural fibre rug under everything. This arrangement seats 5–6 comfortably. The rug is critical — it grounds the seating and defines the space in open-plan homes.</p>
<h3>The feature wall</h3>
<p>Every Mediterranean-Coastal living room should have one feature wall. In order of visual impact:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Textured lime plaster feature wall</strong> — the highest-return, most authentic option. One wall (typically behind the sofa or TV) in a warm-sand or terracotta tone. Cost: ₹280–₹520/sqft including labour.</li>
  <li><strong>Wood-slat feature wall</strong> — vertical solid teak or mango slats floor-to-ceiling. Cost: ₹680–₹1,200/sqft.</li>
  <li><strong>Arched niche wall</strong> — one or three arched wall niches, whitewashed, styled with pottery or plants. Cost: ₹35,000–₹85,000 total.</li>
  <li><strong>Reclaimed wood-beam ceiling</strong> — applied wood beams on the ceiling above the seating area. Genuine reclaimed beams from Rajasthan or Kerala suppliers: ₹1,800–₹4,500/running foot; new wood aged to look reclaimed: ₹850–₹1,800/running foot.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The plants</h3>
<p>Unlike most styles where plants are optional, Mediterranean-Coastal genuinely needs them. Large fiddle-leaf fig, tall bird-of-paradise, olive tree in a matte terracotta pot, trailing pothos on a shelf. Three to five sizeable plants per living room. Real plants — no fake versions. The organic softness completes the aesthetic in a way no decor object can.</p>
<p>Design manager's decision</p>
<h4>The single highest-return intervention</h4>
<p>If the client can afford only one dramatic change in the living room, we pick the textured lime plaster feature wall in a warm sand tone. It costs ₹35,000–₹65,000 for a standard 12x9 foot wall, transforms the entire room's mood, and works in every budget tier. Everything else — the sofa, the rug, the plants — can be scaled up over time. But the wall sets the aesthetic anchor from day one.</p>
<p>10 · Dining Room Design</p>
<h2>Long lunches, honest wood, and one big pendant</h2>
<p>The dining room in Mediterranean-Coastal is designed for the way Southern European households eat — long, unhurried, generational. The furniture supports this. Solid wood tables that can seat six but stretch to eight. Chairs that stay comfortable for two hours. Lighting warm enough to encourage lingering. Nothing about a Mediterranean dining room hurries the meal.</p>
<h3>The table</h3>
<p>Rectangular or oval solid wood table — 180–220 cm long for a 6–8 seater. Live-edge or refectory silhouette. Matte oil finish, never lacquered gloss. Teak is the ideal wood; mango is the workable alternative below premium tier. Expect ₹85,000–₹1.8 lakh for a serious dining table in this style; the ₹35,000 versions from generic retailers will not carry it.</p>
<h3>The chairs</h3>
<p>Two options work equally well:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Wooden cross-back or wishbone chairs</strong> with cane or rush seats — ₹6,500–₹14,000 each. Classic, warm, and durable.</li>
  <li><strong>Upholstered linen dining chairs</strong> with matte black wrought iron frames — ₹8,500–₹18,000 each. Softer, more coastal.</li>
</ul>
<p>Six or eight matching chairs; skip the "different chair at each end" trend — it reads as bohemian.</p>
<h3>The pendant</h3>
<p>The single most important lighting decision in the home. Options:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Rattan or seagrass dome pendant</strong> — 60–80 cm diameter, warm and casual.</li>
  <li><strong>Wood-bead chandelier</strong> — hand-carved wood beads on matte black frame, 70–90 cm diameter.</li>
  <li><strong>Matte black wrought iron fixture</strong> with linen or paper shades — 60–100 cm diameter, more architectural.</li>
  <li><strong>Cluster of three matched pendants</strong> for long tables — matte black frames, opal or paper shades.</li>
</ul>
<p>Hang 75–85 cm above the table surface. Warm 2700K bulbs, dimmable, never LED cool-white.</p>
<h3>The sideboard</h3>
<p>Every Mediterranean-Coastal dining room needs a solid wood sideboard. 180 cm long, waist-height, matte teak or mango finish, matte black hardware. Function is real — serves as buffet, holds tableware, displays ceramic vessels.</p>
<p>11 · Bathroom Design</p>
<h2>Handmade tile, honest stone, and matte black fittings</h2>
<p>Bathrooms in Mediterranean-Coastal homes are unusually good-looking for their cost. The materials — Zellige tile, honed limestone or travertine, matte black tapware, unlacquered wood vanity — are individually accessible, but combined they read as high-end hospitality bathroom. A well-executed Mediterranean-Coastal master bathroom in a Bangalore apartment costs 30–40% less than the equivalent Modern Classic or Deco Revival bathroom.</p>
<h3>The vanity</h3>
<p>Wall-mounted or freestanding wood vanity — matte teak or mango finish, 120–160 cm wide. Honed limestone or travertine slab counter with single undermount ceramic basin, or with a hand-thrown ceramic vessel basin on top. Matte black wall-mounted or deck-mounted tap. Cane or wood-slat drawer fronts add texture; matte solid wood is equally valid.</p>
<h3>Flooring</h3>
<p>Terracotta floor tiles, encaustic cement tiles with a subtle geometric pattern, or honed limestone. All three work; the choice depends on your palette lean (terracotta is warmest, encaustic is most patterned, limestone is most neutral). Never glossy vitrified tiles. Never marble-print laminate. Real material is the point.</p>
<h3>Walls</h3>
<p>Zellige tile on the wet wall behind the vanity and shower — full height, glossy handmade glaze catching light. Remaining walls in warm whitewash textured paint. This combination is authentic to Moroccan and Andalusian tradition and photographs beautifully in Bangalore homes.</p>
<h3>Mirror and fittings</h3>
<p>Arched mirror with matte black frame, or round mirror with rattan-wrap frame. Matte black wall sconces flanking the mirror at eye level. Small rattan or matte black pendant if ceiling height permits.</p>
<h3>The bathtub question</h3>
<p>Freestanding tubs — clawfoot in matte black or slipper-shape in white — are the Mediterranean-Coastal ideal. Practical in villas with generous master bathrooms. Difficult in standard Bangalore 60-sqft apartment bathrooms. If space permits (100+ sqft), a freestanding tub is the room's hero. If not, skip it — a well-designed walk-in shower with Zellige is a better answer than compromising with a built-in tub.</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Compact bathroom tip</span>In a 40–50 sqft compact bathroom, Mediterranean-Coastal still works beautifully. The formula: terracotta tile floor, Zellige wet wall in whitewash or sage green, wall-mounted wood vanity with matte black hardware, arched matte black mirror, matte black wall sconce. Total additional cost over a standard bathroom: ₹55,000–₹95,000.</div>
<p>12 · Lighting Blueprint</p>
<h2>Warm, layered, and never brass</h2>
<p>Lighting in this style follows the same principles as in other premium styles — layered, warm-temperature, dimmable — but with one critical fixture material change. Every decorative fixture must be <strong>rattan, wood, seagrass, matte black wrought iron, or ceramic</strong>. No brass, no gold, no polished chrome. This one material discipline maintains style purity.</p>
<h3>The five-layer rule</h3>
<p>Every primary room needs all five layers:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Ambient</strong> — recessed downlights or cove lighting, warm 2700K, dimmable.</li>
  <li><strong>Task</strong> — reading lamps, kitchen under-cabinet, vanity lights.</li>
  <li><strong>Accent</strong> — narrow-beam spots on art, plants, feature walls.</li>
  <li><strong>Decorative</strong> — rattan pendants, wood-bead chandeliers, matte black sconces. These are the style-defining fixtures.</li>
  <li><strong>Peripheral</strong> — floor lamps beside reading chairs, table lamps on sideboards, small lanterns on shelves.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Signature Mediterranean-Coastal fixtures</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Rattan dome pendant</strong> — over kitchen islands, dining tables, breakfast counters. 40–80 cm diameter.</li>
  <li><strong>Wood-bead chandelier</strong> — hand-carved wood beads on matte black frame. Dining room, foyer, master bedroom.</li>
  <li><strong>Seagrass or paper-shade table lamps</strong> — bedside, sideboard, console.</li>
  <li><strong>Matte black wrought iron sconces</strong> — flanking beds, mirrors, art. Sometimes with linen or paper shades.</li>
  <li><strong>Ceramic table lamps</strong> — hand-thrown or handmade base with linen shade.</li>
  <li><strong>Matte black lanterns</strong> — for hallways, foyer, outdoor covered areas.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Colour temperature discipline</h3>
<p>Every fixture at 2700K throughout the home. Kitchen task lights may go to 3000K but never warmer. Never 4000K or "daylight." The rattan and wood shades on Mediterranean-Coastal fixtures cast warm shadows that only read correctly at 2700K — cooler temperatures make the natural materials look grey and dead.</p>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><span class="callout__title">Most common lighting failure</span>Builder-provided 4000K daylight downlights left in place, even after all decorative fixtures are upgraded. The generic cool overhead light destroys the warmth the decorative fixtures create. Fix: replace every downlight bulb with 2700K dimmable, ₹8,000–₹15,000 for the whole home. Non-negotiable intervention.</div>
<p>13 · Decor, Art &amp; Objects</p>
<h2>Handmade, natural, and never too many</h2>
<p>The Mediterranean-Coastal decor layer is where discipline matters most. The style loves handmade objects — ceramic pots, woven baskets, driftwood pieces, framed botanical prints — and it's easy to over-do them. A room styled with fifteen small objects looks cluttered. A room styled with five larger objects looks curated.</p>
<h3>Art</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Botanical prints or pressed plant frames</strong> — olive branches, ferns, sea grasses. Framed in matte black or bleached wood.</li>
  <li><strong>Abstract watercolours</strong> in coastal palette — muted blues, sand tones, driftwood greys.</li>
  <li><strong>Black-and-white photography</strong> — Mediterranean architecture, coastal landscapes, minimalist portraits. Slim matte black frames.</li>
  <li><strong>Ceramic wall installations</strong> — a cluster of hand-thrown ceramic plates or shallow bowls mounted on a wall as art.</li>
  <li><strong>Single large-scale piece</strong> — one hero artwork above the sofa or bed, rather than a gallery wall. This style prefers one strong statement over many small ones.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Mirrors</h3>
<p>Arched matte black frame mirrors above vanities and consoles. Round rattan-wrap mirrors in bedrooms and powder rooms. Large full-length arched mirror leaning against a bedroom wall. Two to three mirror placements per home; never more.</p>
<h3>Textiles</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Linen and cotton cushions in warm neutrals and one accent tone — never more than two colours per grouping.</li>
  <li>Handwoven throws — cotton, wool, or linen with subtle stripes or geometric weave. Never solid velvet.</li>
  <li>Floor-to-ceiling linen drapery — matte black wrought iron rods, pinch-pleat or ring-top heading. Puddle 1–2 cm.</li>
  <li>Jute or wool rugs — natural fibre, geometric or plain, sized generously.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Objects</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Hand-thrown ceramic vessels — vases, bowls, urns. Terracotta, oatmeal, or matte white.</li>
  <li>Wooden bowls and boards — chunky, matte-finished, live-edge acceptable.</li>
  <li>Woven baskets — seagrass, jute, rattan. Store blankets, magazines, kids' toys.</li>
  <li>Antique or vintage-inspired hardcover books — coffee table styling.</li>
  <li>Real plants — always. Olive tree, fiddle leaf fig, bird of paradise, snake plant, pothos. Terracotta or matte-white pots only.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">The editing rule</span>First pass of styling always overdoes it. Once every horizontal surface is styled, remove one-third of the objects. Then live in the space for two weeks and reassess. Rooms consistently improve after this edit.</div>
<p>14 · Home Types That Suit It</p>
<h2>The honest suitability matrix</h2>
<p>This style is more forgiving than most premium styles when it comes to home type — it works across a wider range of floor plans and ceiling heights. But it has one hard requirement: natural light.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Home type</th><th>Suitability</th><th>Why</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Villas / independent houses</td><td>■■■■■</td><td>Ideal — outdoor connection, generous rooms, garden integration all support the style</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Row houses / duplex apartments</td><td>■■■■■</td><td>Multi-level movement, terrace access, and split-level living align perfectly</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Penthouse apartments</td><td>■■■■■</td><td>Terrace access + high ceilings + natural light = perfect canvas</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Well-lit 3BHK apartments</td><td>■■■■□</td><td>Works beautifully if all primary rooms have windows to a good exposure</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Standard 3BHK apartments (average light)</td><td>■■■□□</td><td>Workable but needs deliberate light-planning — mirrors, sheer curtains, light-coloured floors</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Compact 2BHK apartments</td><td>■■■■□</td><td>Actually works well — style's inherent lightness makes small spaces feel larger</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dark north-facing apartments</td><td>■■□□□</td><td>Difficult — the whitewash palette relies on light; without it, rooms feel dingy</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Heritage buildings with original architecture</td><td>■■■■■</td><td>Perfect — Mediterranean vernacular is exactly what old bungalows already have</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Non-negotiable requirement</span>Natural light. If your primary living space does not get 4+ hours of direct or indirect natural light per day, Mediterranean-Coastal will not work here — the palette relies on real light to bring the warm whites and natural materials alive. In that case, consider Warm Contemporary or Modern Classic instead.</div>
<p>15 · Ideal Room Sizes</p>
<h2>What each room needs to work</h2>
<p>Mediterranean-Coastal is more forgiving of small rooms than most premium styles — the light palette and airy furnishings actually help compact spaces feel larger. These are the minimums:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Room</th><th>Minimum</th><th>Ideal</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Living room</td><td>160 sqft</td><td>240+ sqft</td><td>Deep sofa needs 90 cm clearance in front</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dining room</td><td>120 sqft</td><td>160+ sqft</td><td>6-seater needs 11x11 minimum</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Master bedroom</td><td>120 sqft</td><td>150+ sqft</td><td>King bed + wardrobe + bench needs 12+ ft width</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Kitchen (with island)</td><td>140 sqft</td><td>180+ sqft</td><td>Island needs 90 cm clearance all sides</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Master bathroom</td><td>50 sqft</td><td>80+ sqft</td><td>Freestanding tub needs 90+ sqft</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Foyer</td><td>20 sqft</td><td>35+ sqft</td><td>Console + arched mirror needs presence</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Below these minimums, the style still works — it degrades more gracefully than most premium styles. Compact 2BHKs can carry the full aesthetic if the material and colour discipline is respected.</p>
<p>16 · Who This Style Suits</p>
<h2>Four buyer profiles this style genuinely fits</h2>
<h4>The relaxed family</h4>
<p>Household with kids, dog, casual daily life. Wants a home that looks beautiful but survives real use. Linen slipcovers wash. Solid wood scratches into character. Jute rugs handle spills. This is a family home first, an aesthetic second.</p>
<h4>The travel-loving couple</h4>
<p>Buyers who have spent time in Southern Europe, Greek islands, Californian beach towns, Goa. Recognise the style from lived experience, not just Pinterest. Want the aesthetic they associate with holidays as their daily home.</p>
<h4>The plant-loving minimalist</h4>
<p>Values light, air, natural materials, and negative space over ornament. Comfortable with visible imperfection in materials. Reads design as calmer, not busier, than mass-market contemporary.</p>
<h4>The heritage-property owner</h4>
<p>Owns or inherited a pre-Independence bungalow, an older row-house, or a heritage-listed property. Original arches, terracotta floors, and wood beams are already there. Mediterranean-Coastal is the only contemporary style that respects rather than fights this architecture.</p>
<h3>Buyers this style does not fit</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Households that want their home to look formal or hospitality-grade — this style is deliberately casual.</li>
  <li>Buyers with dark north-facing apartments and limited natural light.</li>
  <li>Homeowners who cannot accept visible imperfection — hand-thrown ceramics have irregularities, real linen wrinkles, terracotta stains.</li>
  <li>Those who dislike plants — the style genuinely needs greenery to complete.</li>
</ul>
<p>17 · Advantages</p>
<h2>Why homeowners choose this style</h2>
<h3>1. Genuinely family-friendly</h3>
<p>Linen slipcovers wash. Solid wood ages beautifully with scratches. Terracotta hides stains. Jute rugs handle spills. This is the most forgiving of all premium interior styles for households with kids, pets, and daily use.</p>
<h3>2. Better cost-to-impact ratio than any other premium style</h3>
<p>The materials are individually cheaper than Deco, Modern Classic, or Contemporary Luxury — but the aesthetic delivered is comparable. A ₹18 lakh Mediterranean-Coastal 3BHK reads as premium as a ₹25 lakh Modern Classic 3BHK. This asymmetry makes it the best-value premium style available.</p>
<h3>3. Suits Indian climate</h3>
<p>Mediterranean architecture was designed for warm climates — thick walls for thermal mass, small windows for heat control, terracotta floors that stay cool. Indian summers, monsoons, and dust patterns align well with this style's material choices.</p>
<h3>4. Ages beautifully</h3>
<p>Solid wood gains patina. Lime plaster develops subtle character over years. Linen softens with washing. Terracotta darkens where feet fall. Zellige tile develops water spots into character. Everything in this style improves visually with time.</p>
<h3>5. Timeless because it's pre-modern</h3>
<p>Mediterranean vernacular is centuries old. Coastal is a hundred years old. Neither is a trend — both are documented enduring styles with continuous demand. This style will not date.</p>
<h3>6. Photographs consistently well</h3>
<p>Warm natural light, textured surfaces, real plants — this style photographs excellently in both natural and warm artificial light. Real-estate listings for Mediterranean-Coastal homes receive higher click-through than any other premium style on Bangalore property platforms.</p>
<p>18 · Disadvantages</p>
<h2>Honest downsides most designers gloss over</h2>
<h3>1. Requires natural light</h3>
<p>The single biggest requirement. Homes with poor exposure, narrow windows, or heavy sun-blocking neighbours will struggle with this style. The whitewash palette needs light to breathe.</p>
<h3>2. Requires real materials</h3>
<p>Every material substitution shows immediately. Faux plaster, laminate wood, printed tile — none of these work. If the budget cannot fund real materials in a room, it's better to do less of the style in that room than to fake it.</p>
<h3>3. Requires visible plant care</h3>
<p>The style needs three to five real plants per living room to complete. Plants die without care. Households without a plant-caring member of the family will end up with either dead plants (visible failure) or fake plants (kills the style).</p>
<h3>4. Zellige tile has real installation risks</h3>
<p>Handmade tile has significant variation, needs specialised sealing, and is fragile during installation. Bangalore tile installers unfamiliar with Zellige regularly damage 15–20% of the supply during installation. Budget +25% overage on Zellige orders.</p>
<h3>5. Linen wrinkles are permanent</h3>
<p>Households that expect furniture upholstery to look ironed will be disappointed. Real linen has creases. Some households find this charming; others find it messy. Know which type of household yours is before committing.</p>
<h3>6. Can slip into "beige bohemian"</h3>
<p>The failure mode of Mediterranean-Coastal is not drama — it's blandness. If the discipline of one accent tone, textured walls, and matte black hardware is not maintained, the home devolves into generic warm neutral with plants. This looks similar in photos to a proper Mediterranean-Coastal home but reads very differently in person. Design discipline matters.</p>
<p>Balanced view</p>
<h4>The trade-off summary</h4>
<p>Every advantage of this style requires a corresponding discipline. Family-friendliness requires accepting scratches and creases. Better cost-to-impact requires real material sourcing. Timelessness requires resisting trendier accents. Homeowners who understand these upfront are consistently thrilled. Those who see the aesthetic and skip the trade-offs are consistently disappointed.</p>
<p>19 · Maintenance Reality</p>
