<h3>Where to overspend</h3>
<p>Textured wall treatment (defines the aesthetic), Zellige tile in kitchen and one bathroom (highest-return single intervention), dining table (solid wood, honest silhouette), rattan or wood-bead statement pendant, plants. These five items carry disproportionate visual weight relative to their cost.</p>
<h3>Where to underspend</h3>
<p>Guest bedroom furniture, secondary bathroom finishes (simple ceramic instead of Zellige is acceptable), and utility area cabinetry. These rooms are seen less often and can use one-tier-lower material specifications without penalty.</p>
<p>23 · Compared With Other Styles</p>
<h2>Where this style sits in the broader design landscape</h2>
<p>Mediterranean-Coastal shares territory with several nearby styles. Understanding the differences is what allows a homeowner (and a designer) to specify one without accidentally slipping into another.</p>
<h3>Mediterranean-Coastal vs Modern Farmhouse</h3>
<p>These two are the closest cousins in the current market. Both use natural materials, both value handmade elements, both suit family living. Modern Farmhouse leans more American — shiplap panelling, barn doors, black-and-white palette, industrial hardware, agrarian references. Mediterranean-Coastal is more Southern European — arched openings, textured plaster, terracotta, wrought iron. Both are relaxed; Farmhouse is rural, Med-Coastal is village-urban.</p>
<h4>Mediterranean-Coastal</h4>
<p>Arches, textured plaster, terracotta, olive tones, Zellige tile, rattan and linen, wrought iron, plants, southern European reference</p>
<h4>Modern Farmhouse</h4>
<p>Shiplap panelling, barn doors, black-and-white palette, exposed brick, industrial hardware, cotton and denim, American agrarian reference</p>
<h3>Mediterranean-Coastal vs Japandi</h3>
<p>Both restrained, both material-honest. Japandi is East Asian (Japanese-Scandinavian hybrid) with lighter woods (oak, ash), lower furniture, more emphasis on negative space and precise geometry. Mediterranean-Coastal is warmer, has more colour tolerance, more ornament permitted, and stronger indoor-outdoor character. Japandi is meditative; Mediterranean-Coastal is convivial.</p>
<h3>Mediterranean-Coastal vs Bohemian</h3>
<p>Bohemian is what Mediterranean-Coastal becomes when discipline is abandoned. Same natural materials, but with pattern layered on pattern, colour piled on colour, tribal art mixed with Moroccan tile mixed with Indian block-print. Bohemian is delightful in small doses and exhausting as a full home. Mediterranean-Coastal is Bohemian with editing.</p>
<h3>Mediterranean-Coastal vs Scandinavian</h3>
<p>Both light-driven, both use natural materials. Scandinavian is cooler in palette (grey-based whites, pale woods, black accents), more geometric, more optimised for northern light. Mediterranean-Coastal is warmer in palette (yellow-based whites, richer woods, terracotta accents), more curved, optimised for warm climates. Same values; different climates and cultures.</p>
<h3>Mediterranean-Coastal vs Contemporary Warm</h3>
<p>Contemporary Warm (as offered by most premium apartment showrooms) uses similar palettes — warm neutrals, wood, some plants — but strips out the specific architectural and material vocabulary. No arches, no Zellige, no terracotta, no textured plaster, no wrought iron. Reads as expensive but generic. Mediterranean-Coastal adds the specificity that Contemporary Warm lacks.</p>
