<p>24 · Common Mistakes</p>
<h2>The traps we see repeatedly in Bangalore projects</h2>
<h3>Mistake 1 — Pure white walls</h3>
<p>Homeowners specify "white walls" and get pure white paint. Under warm LED lighting, this reads cold and hospital-like. The correct specification is a warm off-white with a yellow undertone — Asian Paints "Warm Ivory" or "Cream Cake" range, or specific undertone lime plaster.</p>
<h3>Mistake 2 — Bright brass or gold hardware</h3>
<p>The single most common mistake we see. Homeowners buy expensive brass tapware and hardware, thinking "brass is warm and Mediterranean." It is not. Mediterranean vernacular used wrought iron. Coastal used matte black. Brass belongs in Deco, not here. Any brass or gold immediately fights the aesthetic.</p>
<h3>Mistake 3 — Fake textured paint</h3>
<p>Using flat matte emulsion and calling it "Mediterranean." The style needs visible texture on the walls — even the entry-tier version requires textured paint techniques (Berger Silk Illusions Suede, Asian Paints Royale Play). Flat gyp-board walls in a warm off-white are Warm Contemporary, not Mediterranean-Coastal.</p>
<h3>Mistake 4 — Small rug</h3>
<p>A 5x7 rug that only sits under the coffee table is a decorating error in this style. Rugs need to extend under all seating (an 8x10 minimum for a standard living room). Small rugs make the room look cluttered rather than grounded.</p>
<h3>Mistake 5 — Too many small plants</h3>
<p>Five small pothos plants scattered around the living room. Wrong. This style needs 3–5 <em>large</em> plants — floor-standing olive tree, tall fiddle-leaf fig, big bird of paradise, statement snake plant. Small plants disappear against textured walls.</p>
<h3>Mistake 6 — Cane or rattan everywhere</h3>
<p>Cane wardrobe fronts + rattan chairs + rattan pendant + seagrass rug + jute cushion covers + wicker baskets. Excess kills. Rule: three woven fibre elements per room maximum.</p>
<h3>Mistake 7 — Modern-style crisp linen</h3>
<p>Choosing linen upholstery but expecting it to look pressed and taut like modern furniture. Real linen wrinkles. Slipcovers are meant to look slightly relaxed. If crisp tailoring is the aesthetic priority, choose Modern Classic instead.</p>
<h3>Mistake 8 — Formal drapery headings</h3>
<p>Elaborate pinch-pleat headings with cornices and valances. Wrong. Mediterranean-Coastal uses simple pinch-pleat or ring-top headings on matte black rods. No valances, no elaborate tie-backs, no swags.</p>
<h3>Mistake 9 — White ceiling fans</h3>
<p>Standard-issue white or chrome ceiling fans destroy every bedroom in this style. Wooden-blade or matte-black fans are mandatory. Budget for this upfront; do not treat it as an afterthought.</p>
<h3>Mistake 10 — Buying "Mediterranean" from generic retailers</h3>
<p>Mass-market retailers sell "Mediterranean-style" furniture that is generic beige contemporary with a token wood accent. Almost none of it works. Legitimate Mediterranean-Coastal furniture requires custom fabrication or purchases from specialists — either factory-direct workshops or curated design retailers.</p>
<p>25 · Design Manager Tips</p>
<h2>Twenty things we've learnt from installing this style</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Buy the plants first.</strong> Source large-format plants early — they'll acclimatise to the space during the finishing phase. Empty terracotta pots on delivery day looks unfinished.</li>
  <li><strong>Textured plaster on one hero wall beats textured paint everywhere.</strong> Focus the budget rather than spreading it thin.</li>
  <li><strong>Zellige tile deserves a specialist installer.</strong> General tile installers damage the material. Insist on referrals or in-house specialists.</li>
  <li><strong>Warm off-white paint samples in the actual room light.</strong> The undertone that looks right in the showroom looks blue at home. Test 3–4 shades against real Bangalore afternoon light.</li>
  <li><strong>Matte black tapware from Jaquar Artize or Kohler.</strong> Cheaper matte black hardware peels within 18 months. This is a false economy.</li>
  <li><strong>Solid wood over veneer wherever budget permits.</strong> Solid mango is cheaper than good walnut veneer and looks more authentic to the style.</li>
  <li><strong>Linen slipcovers, not linen upholstery.</strong> Slipcovers wash. Fixed upholstery does not. This is a family-forward style; plan for washing.</li>
  <li><strong>Wall-mounted taps in bathrooms.</strong> Cleaner counter, easier cleaning around basin. Costs ₹2,000 more per basin, worth every rupee.</li>
  <li><strong>One rattan or wood-bead statement pendant per public room.</strong> Never two competing statement pendants in the same visual field.</li>
  <li><strong>Skip the ceiling cove lighting in bedrooms.</strong> Bedrooms in this style read better with simple central fixture + wall sconces. Cove lighting reads modern.</li>
  <li><strong>Live-edge dining tables photograph well but are impractical.</strong> Bark-edge collects crumbs; splintering catches sleeves. Choose classic rectangular slab for daily use, live-edge only if you have a rarely-used dining table.</li>
  <li><strong>Terracotta floor tiles need pre-installation sealing.</strong> Unsealed terracotta absorbs water and stains during grouting. Confirm sealing protocol with installer.</li>
  <li><strong>Ceramic vessel basins are beautiful but harder to clean.</strong> Standard undermount is more practical for main bathrooms; save vessels for powder rooms.</li>
  <li><strong>Buy real linen from D'Decor or Sarom — never generic upholstery shops.</strong> The blend and weight matter enormously to how the fabric ages.</li>
  <li><strong>Arched openings must be structurally reviewed.</strong> Widening a doorway into an arch may involve load-bearing wall considerations. Never skip the structural check.</li>
  <li><strong>Wood-blade ceiling fans in kids' rooms too.</strong> Consistency across the home matters. Standard white fans in kids' rooms undo the aesthetic every time you walk in.</li>
  <li><strong>Skip fake wood beams.</strong> Applied polystyrene "beams" look plastic within a week. Real wood or no beams.</li>
  <li><strong>One arched mirror in the foyer is the highest-return decor purchase.</strong> Under ₹15,000 delivers the biggest single style-signal in the home.</li>
  <li><strong>Fresh flowers or dried grasses on the dining sideboard.</strong> Even a single stem in a tall glass vase makes the room look styled.</li>
  <li><strong>Live in the home for 6 weeks before adding the final 20% of decor.</strong> Real needs become obvious. The pieces bought at that point are always better than those bought at handover.</li>
</ol>
